    "code": "package edu.byuh.mc.cis.cs300.frogameproject.logic;\n\npublic class Player {\n    public static final int X = 0;\n    public static final int O = 1; //fields\n    public static final int BLANK = 2; //edge cases: such as when the game is a tie, or when the game is still ongoing and there is no winner yet, the winner is blank. So we need to have a way to represent that. We can use the constant BLANK to represent that.\n\n    //static means part of class and not part of an object. So you can call this function without creating an object of the class Player. You can call it like this: Player.toString(Player.X) or Player.toString(Player.O) or Player.toString(Player.BLANK). basically if something belongs to class, it is static and accessible without having to create an object. If it belongs to an object, it is not static.\n\n    //void means it does not return anything. It just does something.\n\n    public static String toString(int p) {\n        if (p == X) return \"X\";\n        else if (p == O) return \"O\";\n        else return \"BLANK\";\n    }\n}"
